scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Use the "stack" pattern to space adjacent children uniformly, independent of which elements are present:

```css
.card > * + * { margin-top: 12px; }
/* Remove individual element margins */
.card .title, .card .price, .card .desc { margin: 0; }
```

Explanation: By zeroing specific margins and applying `margin-top` only to elements that have a preceding sibling, every gap is the same (12px) whether `.desc` exists or not. Because the margins are between siblings inside the same formatting context, no container edges are involved and collapse with the card’s edges is avoided. The first child has no top margin by definition of the combinator rule.

Scope the primary nav to its class or position and the breadcrumb to its ARIA label. Example:

```css
/* Primary nav */
nav.primary a:link, nav.primary a:visited { color:#222; font-weight:600; }
nav.primary a:hover, nav.primary a:active { color:#c00; }

/* Breadcrumb */
nav[aria-label="breadcrumb"] li+li::before { content:"/"; margin:0 .5ch; color:#888; }
nav[aria-label="breadcrumb"] a:link, nav[aria-label="breadcrumb"] a:visited { color:#06c; }
nav[aria-label="breadcrumb"] a:hover, nav[aria-label="breadcrumb"] a:active { color:#039; }
```

Justification: Using `nav.primary` and `nav[aria-label="breadcrumb"]` cleanly separates concerns with low specificity, avoids leaking styles to other navs, and respects LoVe/HAte ordering to prevent hover from being trumped by visited. Adding a footer nav later can use `footer nav` or a `secondary` class without touching existing rules.

