scenario_questions.expected_answer:
- Single source of truth: keep one `#sidebar` element in the DOM.
- Use CSS Grid areas (or flexbox with wrap) to reposition without DOM duplication:
```css
.page { display: grid; grid-template-areas: 'sidebar' 'main'; grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
#sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; }
#main { grid-area: main; }
@media (min-width: 992px) {
  .page { grid-template-areas: 'sidebar main'; grid-template-columns: 300px 1fr; }
}
```
- Or with flexbox: keep DOM order sidebar then main; switch from column (mobile) to row (desktop) so visual order matches DOM.
- JS: one toggle tied to the single `#sidebar` (e.g., `sidebar.classList.toggle('open')`). No querying of cloned nodes. Attach the listener once to the button and reference `#sidebar` directly.
- On breakpoint change, normalize state using `matchMedia('(min-width: 992px)')` listeners: on desktop, ensure sidebar is visible (remove `open/collapsed` as needed); on mobile, start collapsed and rely on the toggle.
- Remove the duplicate sidebar from the DOM entirely. If duplication is unavoidable, use `display: none` and do not bind events to hidden clones—but preferred is a single element.
- Justification: grid areas achieve responsive repositioning without changing DOM or `order`, avoiding focus/interaction bugs; one element removes synchronization problems; JS remains simple and robust across breakpoints.

